# Horwall example patterns
# Review before enabling bans: broad rules such as login, graphql, and \.php
# can match legitimate application routes. Remove or narrow them for your site.
# One regex per line. Positive rules match only the request path, without query strings.
#
# Exclusions: prefix a regex with ! to allow matching request paths.
# They take precedence over ALL positive rules, regardless of file order.
# Only the path is checked: query strings are ignored, Referer/User-Agent
# cannot grant an exclusion. Paths are not URL-decoded or normalized.
# Use ^ and $ for an exact path, and \. for a literal dot.
#
# Example: uncomment to allow /allowed.php (also /allowed.php?x=1).
# /other/allowed.php and /allowed.php/extra would NOT be exempt.
# Other PHP paths still match the positive \.php rule below.
# This prevents notifications for this request; it does not lift existing bans.
# !^/allowed\.php$
#
# A bare ! is invalid. Use [!] to match a literal leading ! in a positive rule.
# Restart Horwall after changing the installed pattern file.
#
# No positive rules specifically for these names (NOT explicit exclusions):
# robots.txt
# sitemap.xml
# favicon.ico
# logo.png
# ads.txt
# /.well-known/
^/wp-
\.env
\.git
\.php
wp-json
wp-admin
xmlrpc\.php
graphql
login
config
mcp
sse
\.aws/credentials
phpinfo
administrator
admin/controller/extension
cgi-bin
xleet
\.AWS_/credentials
